My fifth ascension; the first four were a lawful Valkyrie, a lawful 
Archeologist, a chaotic male Valkyrie (just because I could), and a 
neutral male Valkyrie.

In addition to being my first Knight, this was my first attempt at a 
stylish ascension. I was unable, sadly, to ascend with a mount; I lost 
my wished-for ki-rin to a cockatrice in Gehennom and never after found a 
mount more survivable than a warhorse. So my saddle and lance are still 
somewhere in the upper levels of Gehennom.

I think my set of equipment was nonetheless quite stylish. Crystal plate 
mail instead of dragon scale mail was the biggest sacrifice, which made 
staying unburdened quite a challenge. Riding gloves and riding boots 
(which were leather and kicking respectively) rather than speed boots 
and gauntlets also made for a major challenge. I didn't much notice the 
difference between 18/78 and 25 strength, but the lack of extra speed 
was very annoying at times. 

Like any proper knight, I carried a shining shield, which was only a 
problem in that it prevented two-weaponing. (Though I did #enhance 
two-weapon to maximum in the early game). I did have to doff the shield 
in order to have reasonable spellcasting success rates. (Ascended with 
Skilled in Attack and Healing and Basic in Clerical). No T-shirt or 
Hawaiian shirt for the noble knight. 

I carried the legendary Excalibur, but decided that Grayswandir was a 
more appropriate weapon for everyday combat. (Why sully the legendary 
sword with blood?) Finally, a cloak of magic resistance and helm of 
brilliance (which was, appropriately, visored) completed the knightly 
outfit.

This ascension was made while testing my own homebrew patch, which adds 
an additional branch (DramaTech Theater, our campus theater club) to the 
game. This really didn't impact my ascension, except for the handiness 
of the warning bestowed by my new artifact, Clue-by-Four. I also brought 
(but didn't make use of) a deck of cards from the theater for the 
Demigod Bar.

Lack of extra speed didn't really become a problem until I went after 
Rodney, who becomes a lot more dangerous when you're slower. Slogging 
through the Sanctum and the more crowded of the Planes also took a lot 
longer, as in the time it took me to kill one monster, another was 
usually quick enough to move into its place. 

On the way up through the dungeon Rodney double-troubled and/or stole 
various artifacts three or four times, but it was never too terribly 
difficult to stop him. (Being able to controlled-teleport to the 
upstairs was a major help.)

The plane of Earth wasn't particularly noteworthy, though Rodney did 
revive himself once while I was digging around. I went through Air in 
record time after using my only ?oGD to find the portal. (I'd had two, 
but one apparently got misplaced along the way.) 

Fire was the usual crawl, made worse by my low speed - having to page 
through a couple dozen "Fire erupts from the floor under the 
$FIRE_IMMUNE_MONSTER. -more-" and "The blast of fire hits the 
$FIRE_IMMUNE_MONSTER. -more-" messages after every move was very 
tiresome. Water was straightforward - I levitated to the right side of 
the level and zapped my /oSDD, only to find that the portal was in the 
middle of the bubble I was resting in.

On Astral, I went up first, which was a mistake. Pestilence was the 
guardian there, and after dying once, he managed to sicken me three 
times in a row in the space of one of my moves, killing me (my only 
death). Not having extra speed is dangerous! To make things worse, the 
altar was neutral. Finally, adding insult to injury, someone cursed me, 
unblessing my bag of holding. I'd been low on holy water all along, and 
had run out of it along the way trying to keep my unicorn horn, 
luckstone, and bag of holding blessed. Not wanting to run through Astral 
burdened, I pulled out my most important items (Excalibur, a fresh 
amulet of lifesaving, a few lembas wafers, and my last potion of full 
healing) and discarded the bag. You can see it still sitting next to the 
middle altar in the screenshot below.

I backtracked to the intersection, locking the outer door behind me, and 
went west to face Famine. I sensed the death of my guardian Angel along 
the way, sadly. Famine only zapped me once, not even managing to take 
away my slow-digestion-maintained satiation. I paused to kill the Angel 
of Moloch that was poking me with Sunsword, then headed to the (joy!) 
lawful altar. 



